F2FS format utility
---------------------

To use the f2fs filesystem, you should format the storage partition
with this utility. Otherwise, you cannot mount f2fs.

Before compilation
------------------

You should install the following packages.
 - libuuid-devel or uuid-dev
 - autoconf
 - libtool
 - libselinux1-dev
 - xNVMe 0.7.x (libxnvme and its headers), see "CoFDP: PiDir-FS" below

Initial compilation
-------------------

Before initial compilation, autoconf/automake tools should be run.

 # ./autogen.sh

How to compile
--------------

 # ./configure
 # make
 # make install

How to cross-compile (e.g., for ARM)
------------------------------------

 1. Add the below line into mkfs/Makefile.am:
 mkfs_f2fs_LDFLAGS = -all-static

 2. Add the below line into fsck/Makefile.am:
 fsck_f2fs_LDFLAGS = -all-static

 3. then, do:
 # LDFLAGS=--static ./configure \
	--host=arm-none-linux-gnueabi --target=arm-none-linux-gnueabi
 # make

How to run by default
---------------------

 $ mkfs.f2fs -l [LABEL] $DEV

For more mkfs options, see the man page.

CoFDP: PiDir-FS
---------------

This tree adds the mkfs.f2fs part of CoFDP. "mkfs.f2fs -O lost_found"
creates PiDir-FS on an NVMe Flexible Data Placement (FDP) SSD: mkfs reads
the placement identifiers (PIDs) of the device from the FDP Reclaim Unit
Handle Status through xNVMe, sets up the checkpoint slots of the per-PID
logs and creates one Initial P-Directory "p<PID>" per PID in the root
directory, writing its blocks with that PID attached. The CoFDP kernel
enables PiDir-FS from the lost_found feature bit. Without -O lost_found,
mkfs.f2fs creates no P-Directories and does not use xNVMe.

All tools in this tree use the CoFDP checkpoint layout (64 node and 64 data
log slots instead of 8 and 8, see MAX_ACTIVE_LOGS in include/f2fs_fs.h).
Use them only on file systems made by this mkfs.f2fs, and mount those with
the CoFDP kernel (linux-fdp).

 - ./configure fails if libxnvme is not found. "./configure --without-xnvme"
   builds the tools without xNVMe; mkfs.f2fs then rejects -O lost_found.
 - Run mkfs.f2fs -O lost_found on a whole NVMe namespace with a 4 KiB LBA
   format, as the only device (no -c): the Initial P-Directory blocks are
   written with NVMe commands whose LBA is the F2FS block address.
 - mkfs.f2fs -O lost_found fails before writing to the device if xNVMe
   cannot open it, if the Reclaim Unit Handle Status command fails, or if
   the device reports no PID or more than 16 PIDs.
 - PiDir-FS cannot be combined with -O ro, -a 1 (heap-based allocation) or
   -m (zoned devices), and needs at least 131 zones (262 MiB with the
   default zone size) in the main area.

 # mkfs.f2fs -f -O lost_found /dev/nvme0n1
 ...
 Info: PiDir-FS: 8 Initial P-Directories: p0 p1 p2 p3 p4 p5 p6 p7

Known defects of the PiDir-FS layout are listed in ../KNOWN_ISSUES.md.
